From: webmaster@rapidcow.org
Date: Mon, 02 Mar 2026 07:56:32 -0600
Subject: yizx.net DNS migration

Yeah something similar happened when over the
weekend I decided I'm gonna do this migration
to Cloudflare thing I said; this time I didn't
forget any records but I did mis-recall which
of my two VPSes actually runs www.YiZX.net. :x

Noticed this when I saw the UptimeRobot send me
an email about "www.yizx.net DOWN", and because
my sleepy ass only thought "why the hell not lol
and I anticipated less people to be affected by
it (given that I've shared the site with at most
two people and zero of them still talk to me to
this day?), so I went to sleep first instead of
fixing it the first thing after getting back
from Cheesehacks lol

Sorry but I'm just trying to do things at my pace
instead of pleasing non-existent "shareholders"
in my "business".  At this point I'm either out-
sourcing my infrastructure for critical sites
that people depend upon (just pub.rapidcow.org at
this point; I am reclaiming the rapidcow.org for
myself), or you're gonna have to put up with *my*
sleep schedule :P (I really like the concept of a
website that "sleeps", it makes my sleep sounder
knowing my server isn't sucking energy out of the
planet 24/7 (even if that is how VPSes work, I am
considering moving them off because partly because
I want to be physically reminded of the presence
of a computer and the cost of keeping one alive.)
I think taking a break occasionally is a healthy
good promise that doesn't give way to the pressure
that the tips they give about building an online
profile and having an always-on always-connected
server run under your name.  (I just really don't
like this idea about "everyone should build a web
site because it costs you nothing" when you'd know
damn well what it costs if you've ever looked at
the physical power wattage it draws from your wall.
And a web server that exists to serve nothing but
a single page because the person who you have con-
vinced to make a website is uninterested to update
it might as well just be e-waste.  Like, I don't
know, you could say something similar for social
media creating wasteful profiles, even go out as
much length as creating bot accounts to make their
platform look like people are using it.  But for
one we don't have to stoop that low in the first
place simply because the Web is not social media,
and for two... it's just wrong.  Like why do you
care that people do it if it's wrong.  I'm not
saying we should "gate-keep" website-making but
for goodness sake we don't need every person, most
of which uninterested in making a website on the
Web; at least make them be aware of the cost, not
just financially but real down-to-earth resource-
wise costs.  Sometimes I like to believe that the
trouble exists because it forces you to think if
the result is worth the cost, and having that cost
forces you to evaluate if something is actually
worth the cost.))

Anyways another idea I had is that I will be
archiving past updates like these in /feed/&lt;YEAR&gt;/.
I'm beginning to see that I've been (incorrectly)
using this site as a weblog update thingy, and
thought that archiving them as if they were one
is pretty cool. :)
